---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const ohmCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'V', label: 'voltage V', min: 1, max: 24, step: 1, value: 12, unit: 'V' },
    { name: 'R', label: 'resistance R', min: 1, max: 100, step: 1, value: 12, unit: 'Ω' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'current I = V/R', expr: 'V/R', unit: 'A' },
    { label: 'power P = V²/R', expr: 'V*V/R', unit: 'W' },
    { label: 'power via I²R', expr: '(V/R)*(V/R)*R', unit: 'W' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/circuits-and-ohms-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An electric circuit is a closed loop where charge flows. <strong>Voltage</strong> is the push
    (energy per charge), <strong>current</strong> is the flow (charge per second), and
    <strong>resistance</strong> is the opposition — Ohm's law ties them: V = IR. Electrical
    power, the rate energy is delivered, comes out as P = VI = I²R — the reason wires warm, toasters
    toast, and bills arrive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ohm's law as a workbench — drag voltage and resistance, watch current and heat respond" widget="sliderLab" config={ohmCfg} />

  <p>
    Both power readouts agree (P = VI = I²R = V²/R) — pick whichever suits the variables you
    control. Notice the asymmetry: for fixed voltage, halving the resistance doubles current but
    <em>quadruples</em> power. That is why a short circuit (R → 0) is dangerous.
  </p>

  <h2>Series and parallel — the two ways to combine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Series (in a row):</strong> one path; resistances add (R = R₁ + R₂); current is the same everywhere; voltages divide. Old Christmas lights: one bulb dies, all go dark.</li>
    <li><strong>Parallel (side by side):</strong> multiple paths; 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ (total is smaller than the smallest); voltage is shared, currents divide. Your home is wired this way — every appliance gets full mains voltage.</li>
    <li><strong>Kirchhoff's rules</strong> make any circuit solvable: charge in = charge out at every junction (current rule), and voltage gains = voltage drops around any loop (voltage rule).</li>
    <li><strong>Real components:</strong> a battery has internal resistance (it sags under load); wires have small resistance (that's why thin long extension cords get warm).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ohm (1827); OpenStax, College Physics (Current and Resistance, Circuits).</p>
</TopicPage>
